---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import guideImage from '../../assets/images/guides/victoria.jpg';
---

<GuideLayout
  title="Where to start with overnight hikes in Victoria"
  seoTitle="Overnight Hikes Victoria: Beginner Routes and Booking Guide"
  pageImage={guideImage}
  pageImageAlt="Beach at Sealers Cove, Wilsons Promontory National Park"
  description="Overnight hikes in Victoria for beginners: easy routes near Melbourne, bookings, water and toilets, fire-season notes and what to expect."
  eyebrow="Where to go"
  readTime="10 min read"
>
  <p>
    Victoria has some of Australia's most accessible overnight hiking, but
    "accessible" does not mean unregulated. Most hike-in campsites need a
    booking made in advance through Parks Victoria, and several popular routes
    cap group sizes or limit how many consecutive nights you can stay.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Almost every popular overnight hike-in campsite in Victoria needs a
        booking through <a
          href="https://www.parkstay.vic.gov.au/"
          rel="noopener">ParkStay Victoria</a
        > before you leave home.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tidal River to Oberon Bay at Wilsons Promontory and the Great Ocean
        Walk's Apollo Bay to Elliot Ridge leg are the gentlest first overnighters
        in this guide.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sealers Cove's direct track is open again, but it is graded a
        challenging Grade 4 walk, so treat it as a step up rather than a
        first-ever trip.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cathedral Range's Farmyard needs no booking or fee, but it is a harder
        trip: a steep climb in, with no water or toilet at camp. Save it until
        you have tested your <a
          href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">overnight kit</a
        > somewhere easier.
      </li>
      <li>
        Always check current fire ratings and track closures before you go.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <figure class="guide-photo">
    <Image
      src={guideImage}
      alt="Beach at Sealers Cove, Wilsons Promontory National Park"
      widths={[360, 720, 1024, 1400]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 680px"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Photo: <a
        href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sealers_Cove,_Wilsons_Promontory_National_Park_02.jpg"
        rel="noopener">Sealers Cove, Wilsons Promontory National Park</a
      > by Dhx1, public domain (<a
        href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/"
        rel="noopener">CC0</a
      >), via Wikimedia Commons.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What overnight hikes in Victoria need a booking?</h2>
  <p>
    Most Parks Victoria hike-in campsites now require an online booking that
    doubles as your camping permit. Wilsons Promontory's southern circuit
    campsites, including Sealers Cove and Refuge Cove, need <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/places-to-see/sites/sealers-cove-hike-in-camping-area"
      rel="noopener">advance bookings</a
    >, and the <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/places-to-see/parks/wilsons-promontory-national-park/where-to-stay/southern-overnight-hikes"
      rel="noopener">Southern Circuit page</a
    > confirms a mandatory booking, a maximum of two consecutive nights per site and
    a 12-person group cap. The Great Ocean Walk's hike-in campgrounds work the same
    way: a valid booking through ParkStay "acts as your permit to camp," booked per
    campsite per night.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not every site charges or gates entry, though. Cathedral Range State Park's
    Farmyard camping area is a rare exception: the <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/places-to-see/parks/cathedral-range-state-park/where-to-stay/camping-in-cathedral-range"
      rel="noopener">Cathedral Range camping page</a
    > says Farmyard needs no booking and no fee, unlike the park's two car-based
    campgrounds nearby. Check the page for your chosen park before assuming
    either way.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the best overnight hikes near Melbourne?</h2>
  <p>
    "Beginner overnight hike" is relative: even the gentler routes below involve
    a full pack, an unpowered campsite and several hours of walking. Distances
    are one-way, listed roughly from easiest to hardest.
  </p>
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table class="guide-table">
        <thead>
          <tr
            ><th>Route</th><th>One-way distance and time</th><th>How hard?</th
            ><th>Water and toilet</th><th>Booking needed?</th></tr
          >
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Tidal River to Oberon Bay (Wilsons Promontory)</td>
            <td>About 7.6km, about 2.5 hours</td>
            <td>Easy to moderate, well signposted</td>
            <td>Toilet; limited, unreliable tank water (treat it)</td>
            <td>Yes, via Parks Victoria</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Apollo Bay to Elliot Ridge (Great Ocean Walk)</td>
            <td>10km, about 3.5 hours</td>
            <td>Moderate; steep 140m climb near the end</td>
            <td>Toilet, shelter and rainwater tank (treat it)</td>
            <td>Yes, via ParkStay</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cape Otway to Aire River (Great Ocean Walk)</td>
            <td>10km</td>
            <td>Moderate</td>
            <td>Check the campsite page; carry water</td>
            <td>Yes, via ParkStay</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Telegraph Saddle to Sealers Cove (Wilsons Promontory)</td>
            <td>About 10km, about 3 hours (longer on the way back)</td>
            <td>Challenging (Grade 4), steep sections</td>
            <td>Toilet near the beach; carry water</td>
            <td>Yes, via Parks Victoria</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cathedral Range: to The Farmyard</td>
            <td>Short but steep via Jawbone Creek Track</td>
            <td>Hard; not a first trip</td>
            <td>No water, no toilet</td>
            <td>No booking or fee</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Times are for walking with a full pack. Conditions change, so check the
      park page before you go.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Oberon Bay</strong> is the one most people point to for a first
    overnighter. The coastal track from Tidal River passes Little Oberon Bay,
    is well signposted and has no big climbs. There is a creek crossing just
    before camp that is easiest at low tide. Book through the <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/places-to-see/sites/oberon-bay-hike-in-camping-area"
      rel="noopener">Oberon Bay camping page</a
    >, and bring all the water you need, because the tank can run dry.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Apollo Bay to Elliot Ridge</strong> is a one-way walk, so plan how
    you will get back: walk out the same way the next day, book a shuttle, or
    use the V/Line coach to Apollo Bay. Check tide times for the rock and beach
    sections, and pack insect repellent, as hikers often mention mosquitoes at
    camp.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Sealers Cove</strong>: the direct track from Telegraph Saddle
    reopened in September 2026 after a rebuilt boardwalk, so you no longer need
    the long detour via Telegraph Track. It is still graded a challenging Grade
    4 walk with steep sections, and the creek crossing into camp is best done at
    low tide. Check the <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/places-to-see/sites/sealers-cove-walk"
      rel="noopener">Sealers Cove walk page</a
    > before you book, and treat it as a step up rather than a first-ever
    overnight hike.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Farmyard</strong> is free and close to Melbourne, but it is not
    an easy kit test. The shortest way in, the Jawbone Creek Track, is steep, and
    the camp has no water or toilet. Carry all your water (at least 4L per
    person) and a trowel. Stay off the exposed Razorback ridge in wet or windy
    weather.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    A short, well-planned overnight hike with a checked booking beats an
    ambitious one you had to guess your way through.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Multi day hikes in Victoria beyond a first overnighter</h2>
  <p>
    Once a two-day trip feels manageable, Victoria's longer routes step up the
    distance and remoteness rather than the technicality. The <a
      href="https://www.greatoceanroadauthority.vic.gov.au/Land-We-Manage/National-Parks/Great-Otway-National-Park/Great-Ocean-Walk"
      rel="noopener">full Great Ocean Walk</a
    > runs through eight hike-in campgrounds from Apollo Bay to the Twelve Apostles,
    so you can string together as many legs as you have days for. Wilsons Promontory's
    Southern Circuit links Sealers Cove, Little Waterloo Bay, Refuge Cove and other
    coastal camps into a three to five day loop, but every site on it needs its own
    booking and the same permit rules apply throughout. Neither of these is the place
    to improvise; read the relevant Parks Victoria or Great Ocean Road Authority page
    start to finish before you commit dates.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are coming from interstate or comparing options more broadly, our <a
      href="/guides/multi-day-hikes-nsw/">multi day hikes NSW guide</a
    > and <a href="/guides/overnight-hikes-blue-mountains/"
      >Blue Mountains overnight hikes guide</a
    > cover the equivalent routes for New South Wales.
  </p>

  <h2>When is the best time for a multi day hike near Melbourne?</h2>
  <p>
    Coastal routes like the Great Ocean Walk are workable for most of the year,
    though winter brings shorter days and wetter, muddier sections. Alpine and
    inland circuits are a different story: snow can linger into late spring at
    higher elevations, and Victoria's Country Fire Authority runs a Fire Danger
    Period that restricts or bans campfires and, on the worst days, closes parks
    outright. Before locking in dates, check the <a
      href="https://www.cfa.vic.gov.au/warnings-restrictions/fire-bans-ratings-and-restrictions/total-fire-bans-fire-danger-ratings"
      rel="noopener"
      >CFA's current fire danger ratings and total fire ban status</a
    > for the area you are heading to, alongside the specific Parks Victoria page
    for your route.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shoulder seasons, spring and autumn, tend to give the most reliable
    combination of stable weather and open trails for a first attempt. Whatever
    the season, tell someone your planned route and return time. Phone reception
    is patchy at places like Elliot Ridge and the Prom's east coast, so a paper
    map is a good backup.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you book an overnight hike in Victoria?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Search your chosen park on <a
        href="https://www.parkstay.vic.gov.au/"
        rel="noopener">ParkStay Victoria</a
      > and book each campsite and night separately; it doubles as your permit.
    </li>
    <li>
      Book well ahead for school holidays and summer weekends, when popular
      circuits sell out.
    </li>
    <li>
      Carry your booking confirmation, printed or on your phone, and check in
      where the park asks you to.
    </li>
    <li>
      Confirm maximum group size and consecutive-night limits for your specific
      site before you invite people along.
    </li>
    <li>
      Recheck fire danger ratings and any track closures in the days before you
      leave.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What gear do you need for a first overnight hike in Victoria?</h2>
  <p>
    Region guides like this one own the "where"; the gear side is covered in
    depth elsewhere. In short, you are carrying a shelter, a sleep system
    matched to a realistic overnight low, a way to cook, and enough water
    capacity for a site with no reliable tap. Our <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">overnight hike packing list</a
    > runs through the full kit, our <a
      href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
      >sleeping bag temperature ratings guide</a
    > helps you avoid a cold night, and our <a
      href="/guides/2-person-hiking-tent-guide/">two-person hiking tent guide</a
    > covers shelter for a pair. If you would rather start from a complete set, browse
    our <a href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a>. For routes in other states, see
    our <a href="/guides/best-multi-day-hikes-australia/"
      >best multi-day hikes in Australia</a
    > and <a href="/guides/multi-day-hikes-tasmania/"
      >Tasmania multi-day hikes</a
    > guides.
  </p>
  <p>
    Water is the biggest safety variable on Victorian hike-in campsites: many
    rely on untreated rainwater tanks or have none at all, so carry your own
    supply or a reliable treatment method and do not assume a tank will be full.
    Fire rules also change by park and by season; the coastal and alpine routes
    above frequently ban campfires outright and require a gas stove instead.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    For a first night out, start with Oberon Bay or Apollo Bay to Elliot Ridge,
    then work up to Sealers Cove, the Farmyard or a multi-day coastal walk.
    Match the route to your experience rather than the distance alone, book
    as early as you can, and check the specific park page for
    distance, closures and fire rules before you commit to a date. If you're
    missing pieces of your kit, <a href="/build-my-kit/">Build My Kit</a> fills gaps
    around gear you already own.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>What is a good beginner overnight hike near Melbourne?</summary>
      <p>
        Tidal River to Oberon Bay at Wilsons Promontory (about 7.6km, well
        signposted) is the most popular first overnighter. The shorter Great
        Ocean Walk legs, such as Apollo Bay to Elliot Ridge, are another gentle
        option.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do you need a permit for overnight hikes in Victoria?</summary>
      <p>
        Most hike-in campsites do. Wilsons Promontory and the Great Ocean Walk
        both require an advance booking that acts as your permit; Cathedral
        Range's Farmyard camping area needs no booking or fee, though it is a
        harder trip with no water or toilet.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >How far in advance should you book a Victorian hike-in campsite?</summary
      >
      <p>
        As early as your dates allow, especially for school holidays and summer
        weekends. Popular circuits like Wilsons Promontory's southern circuit
        can sell out well ahead of the date.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is Sealers Cove a beginner hike?</summary>
      <p>
        Not really. The direct track reopened in September 2026, but it is
        graded a challenging Grade 4 walk with steep sections, and there is a
        creek crossing into camp best done at low tide. Treat it as a step up
        rather than a first overnight hike.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What are the best multi day hikes in Victoria?</summary>
      <p>
        The Great Ocean Walk and the Wilsons Promontory Southern Circuit are the
        two most established multi-day options, both built around a chain of
        bookable hike-in campsites.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Is drinking water available at Victorian hike-in campsites?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Not reliably. Several Great Ocean Walk camps rely on untreated rainwater
        tanks and other sites have no water at all, so carry your own supply or
        a treatment method and confirm the specific site's facilities before you
        rely on them.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Can you have a campfire on an overnight hike in Victoria?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Often not. Several of the routes above ban campfires and require a
        portable gas stove instead, and fire restrictions tighten further during
        the fire danger period. Check the specific park page and your local fire
        authority before you go.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
